  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nThru tonight...Late morning (15Z) XMR sounding shows drier air in\nthe mid to upper levels with most of the moisture below 725 mb \ncontributing to the calculated 2\" Precipitable Water. Scattered \nshowers and lightning storms will continue to develop and lift \nN/NW. Areas along the coast esp south of the Cape will remain \nmostly dry as activity focuses on the interior and Volusia county.\nThe dry air in the mid levels is contributing to higher downdraft\nCAPE values which supports more gusty wind potential with storms.\nThis drier air aloft is also producing more numerous and well \ndefined outflow boundaries for additional storms to spark. Locally\nheavy rain accumulating 1-3\" will be possible in the slower moving\nstorms.\n\nThe primary threat will be lightning strikes, especially those \nthat occur well outside of the rain shaft. We have seen several \ninstances of this \"blue sky lightning\" so far today. Seek shelter \nin an enclosed building or automobile if thunder is heard or \nthreatening weather approaches. Screened porches, dugouts and \npicnic shelters may keep you dry but they do not protect from \nlightning. \n\nMonday-Saturday...High pressure ridge aloft moves in and holds \nacross the area. The assocd suppression/subsidence will contribute\nto hot conditions and below normal shower and storm chances. \nIsolated to scattered showers will still be possible into Monday \nafternoon/evening. Greatest rain chances (up to 30-40%) will exist\nacross inland portions of the Treasure Coast and Okeechobee \nCounty, with slight rain chances (~20%) elsewhere. For the \nremainder of the week, rain chances currently remain quite limited\naround 20% or less as mid level drier air continues to move into \nthe region and ridge aloft remains in place. A gradual rise in \nrain chances is possible into the weekend, but coverage still \nlooks to be overall below normal. Given the increase in drier air \naloft, while storm coverage remains below normal, any storms that \ndo manage to form will have the potential to become strong, \nproducing strong wind gusts of 40-50 mph and frequent lightning \nstrikes. Weak steering winds will also allow for heavy downpours \nfrom some storms, producing locally heavy rainfall of 1-2\" each \nday. \n\nThese drier than normal conditions will allow for rising heat \nimpacts through the week as little relief will be found from \nafternoon convection. Max temps will reach the low to mid 90s \neach day. Peak heat index values will easily reach 102-107F, and \nwill have to monitor the need for Heat Advisories, especially \nfrom Tuesday onward as portions of the area may see heat index \nvalues rise to 108-110F. Warm and muggy overnight temps in the mid\nto upper 70s will also amplify the overall heat impacts, with \nwidespread Moderate to Major HeatRisk forecast for much of the \narea each day. Continuing to follow heat safety practices will \nremain important as we go through the week.",
